        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>PTFE turns up all over Indian plants: in valve seats, pump seals, flange gaskets, machine bushes and the white tape on every threaded joint. It is chosen because very few materials handle heat, aggressive chemicals and sliding friction at the same time.</p>
<p>It is not a do-everything plastic, though. PTFE is soft, it creeps under sustained load and it expands noticeably with heat. Picking the right product and grade is what separates a part that lasts years from one that leaks in weeks.</p>
<p>This guide covers the main PTFE (Teflon) products, the difference between virgin and filled grades, the limitations to plan for, and a checklist to use before you send an enquiry.</p>

<h2>What is PTFE, and why is it used in industry?</h2>
<p>PTFE (polytetrafluoroethylene) is a fluoropolymer. Teflon™ is a brand name for it. These are the properties that matter to buyers, taken from a typical virgin-PTFE datasheet (<a href="https://www.globaloring.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/TEF.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global O-Ring, PTFE compound TEF</a>):</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Typical value (virgin PTFE)</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Service temperature</td><td>-200 °C to +260 °C</td><td>Works from cryogenic lines to hot process equipment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Chemical resistance</td><td>Practically inert; attacked only by molten alkali metals, chlorine trifluoride and elemental fluorine at high temperature and pressure</td><td>Suits acids, alkalis and solvents found in chemical and pharma plants</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Coefficient of friction (dynamic)</td><td>About 0.06</td><td>Slides with very little friction, without grease</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Density</td><td>2.14–2.18 g/cm³</td><td>Heavier than most plastics, so parts weigh more than nylon equivalents</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hardness</td><td>Shore D 50–60</td><td>Soft: it seals well but deforms under heavy load</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Radiation resistance</td><td>Poor</td><td>Avoid in high-radiation environments</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>These are typical values for comparison. For a critical part, ask for the datasheet of the exact grade being supplied.</p>

<h2>Common PTFE products and where they are used</h2>
<p>Most industrial PTFE parts fall into a few families. The right family depends on whether the part has to seal, slide, insulate or carry fluid.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><a href="/category/teflon-products/ptfe-gaskets-and-flange-gaskets">PTFE gaskets and flange gaskets</a></td><td>Sealing pipe flanges and chemical equipment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PTFE O-rings, seal rings and valve seats</td><td>Valves, pumps and hydraulic systems; leak-tight seats in ball and check valves</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href="/category/teflon-products/ptfe-bushes-and-sleeves">PTFE bushes and sleeves</a></td><td>Low-friction bearings that run without lubrication</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PTFE rods and hollow bars (tubes)</td><td>Stock for machining custom bushes, sleeves, seals and spacers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PTFE tubing, pipe fittings and bellows</td><td>Chemical transfer lines, pharma pipelines, and expansion joints in corrosive service</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PTFE washers and spacers</td><td>Electrical insulation and non-stick spacing in assemblies</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href="/category/teflon-products/ptfe-thread-seal-tape">PTFE thread seal tape</a>, gas tape and packing cord</td><td>Sealing threaded joints, gas lines, valve stems and pump shafts</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href="/category/teflon-products/teflon-hose-separator">PTFE hose separators</a></td><td>Holding parallel hoses apart and clamped in place</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Virgin vs filled PTFE: which grade do you need?</h2>
<p>This is the most important choice, and listings often skip it. Fillers are blended into PTFE to fix its weak points (creep and wear), at the cost of some purity and chemical resistance.</p>

<h3>Virgin PTFE</h3>
<p>Pure, white PTFE. It has the widest chemical resistance and the best electrical insulation, and it is the usual choice for food and pharma contact. Its weakness is mechanical: it creeps and wears faster under load.</p>
<p><strong>Use it for:</strong> gaskets in aggressive chemicals, seals, insulating washers, and food or pharma parts.</p>

<h3>Glass-filled PTFE</h3>
<p>Glass fibre gives good wear resistance, low creep and good compressive strength. It is abrasive to mating surfaces, especially in rotary applications, so check the shaft hardness (<a href="https://www.processingmagazine.com/process-control-automation/instrumentation/flow-measurement/article/55349193/7-fillers-to-enhance-the-performance-of-virgin-ptfe-sealing-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Processing Magazine, PTFE fillers</a>).</p>
<p><strong>Use it for:</strong> seals, piston rings and guides running against hardened surfaces.</p>

<h3>Carbon- and graphite-filled PTFE</h3>
<p>Carbon adds wear resistance and good thermal conductivity, and carbon fibre gives better creep resistance than carbon powder. It is less abrasive than glass. Graphite is another common filler in this family.</p>
<p><strong>Use it for:</strong> bushes and seals that need wear resistance, and dry-running parts that build up heat.</p>

<h3>Bronze-filled PTFE</h3>
<p>Bronze gives excellent wear resistance and thermal conductivity for heavily loaded parts. It has higher friction than other filled grades and poorer chemical resistance than other PTFE compounds, so keep it out of corrosive media (same source).</p>
<p><strong>Use it for:</strong> hydraulic guide rings and high-load bearings in clean, oil-lubricated systems.</p>
<p>Filled grades are often coloured: black or grey (carbon or graphite), beige (glass) or bronze-brown. See our <a href="/category/teflon-products/filled-ptfe-bushes-and-tubes">filled PTFE bushes and tubes</a>.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Grade</th><th>Main strength</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Virgin</td><td>Purity, chemical and electrical resistance</td><td>Creep and wear under load</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Glass-filled</td><td>Wear resistance, low creep, compressive strength</td><td>Abrasive to mating surfaces</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Carbon/graphite-filled</td><td>Wear resistance, conducts heat</td><td>Confirm suitability if insulation is needed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bronze-filled</td><td>High load, heat transfer</td><td>Higher friction; poor in corrosive media</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Limitations to plan for</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Creep (cold flow):</strong> under sustained compression, PTFE slowly deforms. Gaskets can lose bolt load, so retighten as the manufacturer advises or use a filled or envelope design.</li>
  <li><strong>Thermal expansion:</strong> PTFE grows much more with heat than steel does. Bushes need extra running clearance, and tight press fits can loosen or seize as temperature changes.</li>
  <li><strong>Softness:</strong> it scratches and deforms easily, so it is a poor choice for high-impact or abrasive slurry duty unless it is filled.</li>
  <li><strong>Radiation:</strong> resistance is poor, so avoid it near strong radiation sources.</li>
  <li><strong>Bonding:</strong> PTFE is non-stick, so glues do not hold well. Plan for mechanical fixing such as clamping, press fits or threads.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to choose the right PTFE part: a buyer's checklist</h2>
<p>Share these details with your supplier and you will get a better recommendation and a more accurate quote:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Media:</strong> exactly which chemicals, steam, water or oils the part will touch, and their concentration.</li>
  <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> the normal and peak operating temperatures.</li>
  <li><strong>Load and motion:</strong> is the part static (a gasket) or sliding or rotating (a bush)? Give the approximate load, speed and shaft material.</li>
  <li><strong>Hygiene:</strong> for food or pharma contact, ask for a food-contact compliance declaration for the grade. PTFE resins can meet <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/177.1550" target="_blank" rel="noopener">US FDA 21 CFR 177.1550</a>, but fillers change this.</li>
  <li><strong>Electrical needs:</strong> does the part need to insulate, or dissipate static?</li>
  <li><strong>Dimensions and tolerance:</strong> send a drawing or a sample, and mention the mating part.</li>
  <li><strong>Quantity:</strong> a one-off replacement and a repeat batch may be made differently, for example machined from rod or tube versus moulded.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Two questions buyers often ask</h2>
<h3>Thread seal tape vs gas tape</h3>
<p>White PTFE thread seal tape is for water and general plumbing joints. Yellow gas tape is thicker and denser, and its colour helps inspectors recognise gas-rated tape. Gas-line tape should carry certification to the standard your project specifies, such as <a href="https://knowledge.bsigroup.com/products/bs-en-751-3-sealing-materials-for-metallic-threaded-joints-in-contact-with-1st-2nd-and-3rd-family-gases-and-hot-water-part-3-unsintered-ptfe-tapes-and-ptfe-strings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BS EN 751-3</a>. Check the product's own certificate rather than relying on colour.</p>

<h3>PTFE or nylon?</h3>
<p>PTFE does not absorb water and handles higher temperatures and harsher chemicals. Nylon is usually cheaper and stiffer, but it absorbs moisture and can swell, which affects tight tolerances. For wet, hot or chemical duty, PTFE or a filled PTFE is normally the safer choice.</p>

<h2>PTFE hose separators</h2>
<p>Where several hoses run side by side, a hose separator keeps them spaced and clamped, which reduces rubbing and tangling. Aditya Enterprises makes AEM-make Teflon hose separators in standard 53 mm (AEM/TH53), 43 mm (AEM/TH43) and 36 mm (AEM/TH36) sizes, and in custom sizes. See the <a href="/category/teflon-products/teflon-hose-separator">Teflon hose separator</a> page for details.</p>

<h2>Sourcing PTFE parts from Aditya Enterprises</h2>
<p>Our <a href="/category/teflon-products">Teflon (PTFE) products range</a> covers tubing, pipe fittings, rods, hollow bars, bushes, gaskets, O-rings, washers, valve seats, bellows, thread and gas tape, packing cord and filled-PTFE parts. Our <a href="/category/machining-work">machining shop</a> works in engineering plastics including PTFE, so non-standard parts can be machined from your drawing or sample.</p>
<p>Not sure which grade fits your application? <a href="/contact-us">Contact our team</a> with the media, temperature, load and size, and we will help you choose the right PTFE part.</p>

<h2>References</h2>
<ul>
